  data [label="Full dataset", fillcolor="#BDE0FE"];
  search [label="Hyperparameter search\n(candidate configurations)", fillcolor="#CDEAC0"];
  repeat [label="For each candidate\nconfiguration", shape=oval, fillcolor="#DCCEF9"];

  data -> repeat;
  search -> repeat;

  subgraph cluster_cv {
    label="K-fold cross-validation (example: k = 3)";
    style="rounded,filled";
    color="#8ECAE6";
    fillcolor="#EAF6FB";

    train1 [label="Train on folds 2–3", fillcolor="#A8DADC"];
    eval1  [label="Validate on fold 1", fillcolor="#FFB4A2"];
    score1 [label="Fold 1 score", fillcolor="#FFE29A"];

    train2 [label="Train on folds 1 & 3", fillcolor="#A8DADC"];
    eval2  [label="Validate on fold 2", fillcolor="#FFB4A2"];
    score2 [label="Fold 2 score", fillcolor="#FFE29A"];

    train3 [label="Train on folds 1–2", fillcolor="#A8DADC"];
    eval3  [label="Validate on fold 3", fillcolor="#FFB4A2"];
    score3 [label="Fold 3 score", fillcolor="#FFE29A"];

    train1 -> eval1 -> score1;
    train2 -> eval2 -> score2;
    train3 -> eval3 -> score3;

    { rank=same; train1; train2; train3; }
    { rank=same; eval1; eval2; eval3; }
    { rank=same; score1; score2; score3; }
  }

  repeat -> train1 [label="same candidate"];
  repeat -> train2;
  repeat -> train3;

  aggregate [label="Aggregate fold scores\n(mean / standard deviation)", fillcolor="#FFD6A5"];
  score1 -> aggregate;
  score2 -> aggregate;
  score3 -> aggregate;

  compare [label="Compare candidates", shape=diamond, fillcolor="#E4C1F9"];
  best [label="Select best\nhyperparameters", fillcolor="#B7E4C7"];

  aggregate -> compare -> best;
}
```

Render it with `dot -Tpng kfold_tuning.dot -o kfold_tuning.png`.

## Evaluation

Assistant B’s answer is better. Its DOT script is usable and presents the essential cross-validation idea: each fold has a training portion and a held-out validation portion, and the fold results are aggregated before selecting hyperparameters. It is also reasonably concise.

Assistant A proposes a more elaborate diagram, but its DOT code contains syntax errors: several node and edge attribute lists use semicolons before the closing bracket, which can prevent Graphviz from parsing the file. It also uses some unsupported or misspelled attributes and shape names, so even after syntax fixes, parts of the intended styling may not render. Conceptually, calling each held-out fold a “Test Set” is also less appropriate for tuning than “Validation Set,” since a final test set should generally be kept separate from hyperparameter selection.

B’s main limitation is that it shows only one hyperparameter set, even though tuning requires evaluating multiple candidate configurations. Its train-to-validation arrows are also a simplified representation of training and evaluation. The response would be stronger if it explicitly showed the candidate configurations being repeated through the folds, as in my example. Still, it provides valid, colourful Graphviz code and answers the request more reliably than A. My final verdict is: [[B>>A]]

